          "image_source": "images/page_16_chart_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.455,
            0.885,
            0.523
          ],
          "content": "When the frequencies are larger and we cannot use the scale of 1 unit length = 1 number (frequency), we need to choose a different scale like we did in the case of pictographs."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.536,
            0.884,
            0.581
          ],
          "content": "**Example**: The number of runs scored by Smriti in each of the 8 matches are given in the table below:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.17,
            0.596,
            0.879,
            0.686
          ],
          "content": "| Match | Match1 | Match2 | Match3 | Match4 | Match5 | Match6 | Match7 | Match8 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Runs | 80 | 50 | 10 | 100 | 90 | 0 | 90 | 50 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_table_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.706,
            0.887,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": "In this example, the minimum score is 0 and the maximum score is 100. Using a scale of 1 unit length = l run would mean that we have to go all the way from 0 to 100 runs in steps of l. This would be unnecessarily tedious. Instead, let us use a scale where 1 unit length = 10 runs. We mark this scale on the vertical line and draw the bars according to the scores in each match. We get the following bar graph representing the above data."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.086,
            0.941,
            0.114,
            0.959
          ],
          "content": "I 90"
        },
